    open_questions: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }
  }
}

const PREAMBLE = `You are an evidence-gathering agent for an ARCHITECTURE DESIGN task on the "Codex Dock" system. Do NOT edit anything. Read REAL files and cite exact paths + line numbers.

PRODUCT CONTEXT (verify/refine against repo): Codex Dock is an iPhone app that is a fast personal operations dashboard for THOUSANDS of "Codex" (AI coding agent) threads running across MULTIPLE hosts. A relay server streams thread state to the iOS client. The user wants reliable newest-first ordering, useful per-row summaries/labels, resilient connectivity, archive + detail workflows, and live updates — WITHOUT pushing massive raw history payloads to the phone (bounded first payloads + incremental updates are sacred).

CURRENT FAILURE: "codex.clientOrderRootCause" — a thread-ordering bug where the client's row order is wrong/drifts.

DESIGN GOAL the architects will pursue: make the row/list/detail data CONTRACTS drift-proof BY CONSTRUCTION (single source of truth, one canonical ordering source, one projection path), not by lint. Preserve performance. A large refactor is acceptable.

WORK ROOT: /Users/aelaguiz/workspace/codex-client
UPSTREAM CODEX SOURCE: /Users/aelaguiz/workspace/codex

Return TIGHT structured findings — facts, paths, line refs, contracts, drift risks. NOT file dumps. Be precise about WHERE truth is defined and WHERE it is re-derived (that is the drift surface).`

phase('Explore')

const TASKS = [
  {
    label: 'product-ux',
    task: `Infer the COMPLETE intended user experience from repo docs. Read: README.md, docs/CODEX_DOCK_GOALS_2026-05-29.md, docs/CODEX_DOCK_LLM_THREAD_CARD_LABELS_2026-05-30.md and its _WORKLOG, and any other product/vision/goals docs you find under docs/. Answer: What screens exist (thread list, detail, archive, host views)? What does a single row/card show (title? labels? status? last activity? host?)? What ordering does the user expect and on what key? What are connectivity/resilience expectations (reconnect, offline, live updates)? What scale and multi-host model? What are the explicit non-negotiable performance promises stated in docs? Produce a crisp model of the product so an architect who never saw the app understands what it is FOR.`
  },
  {
    label: 'root-cause',
    task: `Deeply understand codex.clientOrderRootCause. Read docs/CODEX_DOCK_CLIENT_ORDER_ROOT_CAUSE_2026-05-30_WORKLOG.md in FULL. Then trace the ACTUAL code paths it implicates on BOTH relay and client. Pin down: where is thread ordering computed? what key is ordering based on (created? updated? last-event ts? sequence number? arrival order?)? where does the client re-sort or trust server order? where do snapshot order and incremental-update order disagree? Identify EVERY place an ordering decision is made and whether they agree. State the precise root cause in mechanism terms, and what invariant was violated.`
  },
  {
    label: 'client-projection',
    task: `Map the iOS/Swift client data + projection path. Find the Swift models for threads, the list/rows, and the detail view. Trace: wire bytes -> decoded DTO -> client domain model -> row/card view model -> SwiftUI view, and the detail path. Where is each transformation? Is there ONE projection from model->row, or is row-summary logic duplicated (e.g. label/title/status computed in multiple spots)? How is the in-memory thread collection held and mutated (array? dict? ordered store?)? Where is sort applied on the client? How are incremental updates applied to the in-memory store? Identify the client-side contracts and every duplication of projection/ordering logic. Use Glob/Grep to find *.swift sources.`
  },
  {
    label: 'relay-state-engine',
    task: `Map the relay-side state engine (recent commit "relay state engine hard cutover" / "cddf824"). Find where the relay tracks per-thread state, builds the subscribe SNAPSHOT, computes ordering, and emits INCREMENTAL updates. Answer: what is the relay's source of truth for thread state and order? Is ordering computed once and shared, or recomputed for snapshot vs delta? What fields does the relay maintain per thread? How does it decide what goes in the bounded snapshot vs streamed later? Cite the engine files and the snapshot/delta construction sites.`
  },
  {
    label: 'wire-contract',
    task: `Map the WIRE contract between relay and client. Find the message/event/protocol type definitions (Codable structs, JSON shapes, enums of event types). What is in a subscribe snapshot vs an incremental update vs a detail fetch? How is a row represented on the wire (what fields)? How is ordering conveyed (explicit index/sequence? sort key? or implied by array order)? CRUCIAL: is the wire contract defined ONCE and shared by relay+client, or are there TWO parallel definitions (server-side and Swift-side) that must be hand-kept in sync? Cite both sides if duplicated. This is the central drift surface.`
  },
  {
    label: 'performance-machinery',
    task: `Find and explain the PERFORMANCE machinery that must NOT be lost. Look at recent commits: "client responsiveness hard cut" (7e67177), "Bound Dock subscribe snapshots" (reverted, 70c1f0e), and related. Answer precisely: what bounds the first/subscribe payload size (count cap? byte cap? window?)? How are large thread histories kept OFF the phone (lazy detail fetch? truncation? summaries only in list)? How do incremental updates work (full re-snapshot vs targeted deltas/patches; per-thread vs per-collection)? What makes the client "responsive" (debounce, diffing, main-thread batching)? Document the exact invariants/limits so the new architecture preserves them.`
  },
  {
    label: 'upstream-protocol',
    task: `In /Users/aelaguiz/workspace/codex (UPSTREAM), find the Codex thread/session/rollout EVENT protocol that the relay consumes upstream. What events define thread identity, timestamps usable for ordering (created/updated/last-activity), titles/summaries, and lifecycle/state? What is the canonical ordering signal available from upstream? This tells us what RAW truth exists at the source before the relay projects it. Cite protocol/event/schema files. Use Glob/Grep across that repo; it is large, so target protocol/proto/event/session/rollout definitions.`
  },
  {
    label: 'proof-and-prior-art',
    task: `Two jobs. (1) PROOF SURFACE: find existing tests that exercise the stream lifecycle — subscribe snapshot, incremental updates, ordering, projection. Where are they, what do they actually assert, and do they exercise the SAME stream lifecycle the app uses or a parallel fake? (2) PRIOR ART: read EVERYTHING under .arch_skill/model-consensus/dock-contract-architecture-20260530T205733Z/ (this is a prior consensus run on THIS EXACT topic) and any existing architecture docs under docs/. Summarize prior architectural proposals, decisions, signoff criteria, and any rejected ideas — so we do not repeat work. Cite files.`
  },
]

const findings = await parallel(TASKS.map(t => () =>
  agent(PREAMBLE + '\n\n## YOUR ASSIGNMENT: ' + t.label + '\n\n' + t.task, { label: t.label, phase: 'Explore', schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA })
))

return findings.filter(Boolean)

